--------------------------------------------------------------------------
README file for fluxCalc component of OOSSIM
Created Sept 25, 2008 by Thomas Johnson
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

The makespec program is designed to calculate flux received from a star
given its apparent magnitude, effective temperature, metallicity, and a
host of parameters regarding the telescope design: diameter, exposure
time, pitch(arcseconds per pixel), mirror coating, detector type, mirror 
geometry and bandpass filter parameters. The output of the program, sent
to standard output, is the number of incident photons.

INSTALLATION--------------------------------------------------------------

1. Ensure you have either f77 or g77 installed as a FORTRAN compiler. 
   This can be tested with the command "which f77" or "which g77".
	- If neither exists on your machine, go to 
	  www.gnu.org/software/fortran/fortran.html to download the 
	  g77 compiler.
	- Be sure to install it into a directory contained in the PATH
	  environment variable

2. Open 'fluxCalc/Makefile' and uncomment the lines pertaining to the
   compiler you installed. I.e. there are two lines for f77 and two for
   g77. Which lines you use depend on which compiler you installed.

3. In the fluxCalc directory type "make" to compile the programs. If
   all goes well, you should see a bunch of .o files in the fluxCalc
   directory. If not, ensure the compiler has been installed properly
   and is in a PATH directory.

4. In your shell configuration file, set the environment variable
   FLUXCALC to the directory you have installed the program in. E.g.
   if the program resides in /working/oossim/fluxCalc/ and you use bash as
   your shell, add the following line to ~/.bashrc:
	export FLUXCALC=/working/oossim/fluxCalc


---------------------------------------------------------------------------

For detailed workings on the program, please visit the website of the
primary programmer, Steven Gwyn: 

	http://www.astro.uvic.ca/grads/gwyn/ngst/docuphot.html

The sections on flux-calculations are the only ones relevant as the
makespec utility was a component of a larger suite of utilities which have
not been used here.

RELEVANT END-USER INFORMATION----------------------------------------------

1. The various parameters of the simulated telescope are all laid out in
   the makespec.par.hard and makespec.par files. the "hard" variables are
   the ones that are specific to the telescope, and will not change during
   the Occultation Simulation. The non-hard variables are the stellar type
   (or effective temperature) and the visual magnitude.

2. All of the relevant libraries of data are contained in the data folder.
   Specifics: QE of CCD detector, flux vs. wavelength tables for different
   stellar types, reflectivities of different primary mirror materials.
   Wavelengths are in microns and response functions are in fractions.

3. The current set-up is to use Teff instead of the stellar type, with the
   corresponding information contained in the t#####m00.norm.form files. The
   library comes from LeJeune et al., 1997.

4. If one wishes to simply run the fluxCalc on its own, the program 
   'makespec' is what does the actual calculation. Note that one must first
   specify the variables in makespec.par.hard and makespec.par before 
   running. The output will be printed as a simple exponential number,
   representing the number of photons incident on the detector per frame
   (i.e. integrated over the exposure time).

---------------------------------------------------------------------------